{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 ignores the required second part of the problem: 'Identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer.' The target's mechanism explains why reliability might improve (cognitive friction converting recognition into verification), but provides no evidence criterion to distinguish genuine reliability from coincidental checklist compliance. The counterconditions address when the claim fails, not how to tell apart genuine success from rote compliance. Omitting this distinguishing-evidence analysis is a substantive failure against the problem as stated.","counterexample":[{"type":"missing_distinguishing_evidence","detail":"Suppose the checklist items map trivially onto surface cues that correlate with the answer (e.g., 'If keyword X appears, mark Y'). The panel follows it perfectly and tracks the right answer, but this is mere compliance, not a reliability gain. SRC_001's mechanism offers no within-evaluation evidence to separate this case from genuine cognitive friction; its counterconditions concern failure modes, not the compliance-vs-genuine distinction."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}